Description: Steady one-decimal BPM readout, and a deck tempo that matches it
 The deck's BPM number would not sit still: it drifted a few hundredths up and
 down as a track played, which is unreadable at a glance and useless for
 comparing two decks. Three changes, and the last two only make sense once the
 first is in place.
 .
 1. The cause. Rekordbox stores each beat's time rounded to a whole
    millisecond -- 44 frames at 44.1 kHz -- and Beats::fromBeatPositions()
    treats two consecutive beats as the same tempo only while their lengths
    agree to within one frame. A perfectly steady track therefore arrived as a
    variable-tempo grid with a tempo marker at nearly every beat, and
    BpmControl's eight-beat local-BPM window walked up and down with the
    rounding. But the file also records the tempo at each beat, as BPM x 100,
    and when that never changes the track really is steady: the grid is then
    rebuilt from that exact tempo, anchored on the mean of the rounded beats
    so the rounding averages out, leaving it within half a millisecond of
    rekordbox's own grid. Grids that really do change tempo are untouched.
 .
 2. The readout shows one decimal place, and VisualsManager writes visual_bpm
    in whole 0.1 steps with a little hysteresis, so a deck that is synced or
    playing a genuinely drifting grid cannot flicker between two numbers
    either.
 .
 3. The tempo itself moves in the same 0.1 BPM steps. Rounding the display
    alone would put two decks on the same number while they ran up to 0.05 BPM
    apart, which is the reading that matters most. So the pitch fader now
    lands on a rate at which the deck's BPM is a whole step
    (BpmControl::snapRateRatioToBpmStep), and a freshly loaded track is put
    back on the grid once its local BPM is known -- otherwise it would inherit
    whatever rate the previous track left behind. Only those two user-driven
    paths snap: sync writes rate_ratio directly and is left alone, and the
    leader it follows is on the grid already. The fader is 14-bit, about 16000
    positions across the range, so one step is far finer than it can be placed
    by hand.
 .
 Four EngineSyncTest cases read a deck's BPM or rate back and assert the
 unstepped value, so they are updated rather than left failing: the three that
 build a beatgrid from kDivisibleBpm (44100/344, about 128.1976, chosen for
 whole-sample beat positions rather than for a round BPM) now expect the
 128.2 the deck lands on, and SyncWithoutBeatgrid -- which exists to prove
 enabling sync does not *reset* the rate -- allows the half-step the slider
 moves when sync writes it back from the stepped ratio, about 0.001 of its
 travel, instead of demanding the rate be untouched to the last bit.
