+            // calls two consecutive beats the same tempo only while their
+            // lengths agree to within one frame, so those rounded times put
+            // a tempo marker at very nearly every beat and a perfectly
+            // steady track arrives as a variable-tempo grid. BpmControl then
+            // measures the deck's BPM over an eight-beat window, and the
+            // rounding walks that measurement a few hundredths of a BPM up
+            // and down as the track plays: that wobble is what the deck's
+            // BPM readout showed.
+            //
+            // So where the file says the tempo never changes, build the grid
+            // from that exact tempo instead of from the rounded times. The
+            // anchor is the mean of the rounded beats measured against it,
+            // so the per-beat rounding averages out over the track instead
+            // of the whole grid hanging off whichever millisecond the first
+            // downbeat happened to land in, and the result stays within half
+            // a millisecond of rekordbox's own grid everywhere. Grids that
+            // really do change tempo keep their per-beat positions.
